<p>The Dutch-flagged expedition cruise ship MV Hondius left waters off Cape Verde for Spain&#x27;s Canary Islands after three people with confirmed or suspected hantavirus infections were evacuated, while nearly 150 passengers and crew remained aboard, isolated in their cabins. The World Health Organization said an outbreak linked to the ship had resulted in three deaths and eight confirmed or suspected cases; testing identified the Andes virus, a hantavirus strain found mainly in Argentina and Chile that can, in rare cases, spread between people. Spain&#x27;s national government said the ship could dock in Tenerife, while Canary Islands authorities criticized Madrid&#x27;s decision and said it had been imposed without consultation. Health authorities were tracing contacts internationally, including 23 passengers who disembarked at Saint Helena two weeks earlier, and cases or monitoring efforts were reported in Switzerland, South Africa and the United States among people linked to the voyage. Officials in Argentina, where the cruise began in Ushuaia, were investigating whether exposure occurred before boarding as they examined possible links to the country&#x27;s hantavirus activity.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/v2aaWwnDCvJ6EA6H_C9CmvZo3R9Q5qVZeDHg5Rl_YnqBRqNm5SMOxdDerIDilQDoXLsvoYzFqk8nOw7t6ymyPyeCROTuo0_Zrl8JT27_Cig0gTu0E70Rv0GQ1Lzn0YkPNHsuYG0yrpt1lH8jbxV7WJsXF07ThcX4cR57yJOOJirfsquxbmulLB9fOESYzWrfLrOJlT4' alt='The MV Hondius at the port of Praia, Cape Verde, on May 4.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Route context: The MV Hondius was repositioning north for its Arctic season after completing Antarctic voyages, following a remote Atlantic route that complicated port options after illness was recognized.</li><li>Evacuee identity: Reports identified one of the evacuated patients as British expedition guide Martin Anstee, 56, who had been working aboard the MV Hondius.</li><li>On-ship case status: In a Wednesday update, WHO said no one else on the vessel was showing symptoms at that point, even as monitoring continued.</li><li>Clinical severity: Health agencies cited in reports said severe cases can progress from fever and gastrointestinal symptoms to pneumonia, acute respiratory distress and shock.</li><li>Onboard conditions: Passengers described fear and boredom while medical workers in protective suits tended to the sick and the operator looked for a safe port.</li></ul><blockquote>People are taking the situation seriously but without any panic, trying to keep social distancing and wearing masks to be safe, - Kasem Hato</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>World Health Organization: Maria Van Kerkhove said WHO was investigating whether infections among the closest onboard contacts — including spouses and cabinmates — represented rare secondary spread, rather than a single source. (<a href='https://www.mprnews.org/story/2026/05/06/npr-cruise-ship-hantavirus'>MPR News/NPR</a>)</li><li>Spanish national government: Madrid said it was organizing a health operation with European Union coordination after granting the Dutch-flagged ship permission to dock in Tenerife. (<a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/environment/article/2026/05/07/hantavirus-madrid-and-the-canary-islands-clash-over-arrival-of-mv-hondius-in-tenerife_6753200_114.html'>Le Monde</a>)</li><li>Canary Islands authorities: The regional government opposed receiving the vessel and asked that medical treatment and repatriations take place from Cape Verde instead. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1998267/plane-with-hantavirus-ship-evacuees-lands-in-canaries'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Argentina investigators: Officials and experts were reviewing whether early patients had been exposed during travel in Argentina before embarkation, including time spent in Ushuaia and other areas where hantavirus is endemic. (<a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/hantavirus-rise-argentina-stricken-cruise-ship-began-journey-132717110'>ABC News</a>, <a href='https://